	Welcome SysOp! This is yet another fine program I've managed to
release. The goal, create online pfiles that are worth playing. The online
games offer one thing that callers cannot get on their own computers.
Interaction amongst several players. To be successful, a program must be
made with this in mind. Also the game must be progressive. It must have a
life cycle to it and a goal to be achieved. Such was the case with my first
program, Wall Street Blues. Players competed against each other for the
highest total for the month. It was not a perfect game, only my first. I
learned alot from it. I was in a hurry to release that game and it went out
needing several revisions due to bugs. This game has been in the works for
over a year now. It's very complicated and it was a devil to program.
	As always, I'm very interested in bug reports, and suggestions for
improvements. Please send them to VTCaesar@aol.com. If the response is good 
on this game I may consider improving it's play format such as, money 
accounts, hall of fame lists, and any ideas received from others. ANSI 
screens, I'm not very good at these. If you can improve on any of my ANSI 
screens, main menu and such, I may consider using them. Send any you make
to my internet e-mail address and I'll include them in future revisions.

	Version 2.0 is looking great! It's so much easier to improve
something than to start fresh. I'm still interested in improved ANSI
screens if you know any good ANSI artists.

UPGRADE INSTALLATION:
	Copy all files in this archive over all the old files. Everything
should run under the old cnet settings.

NEW INSTALLATION:

	There were problems with the installation of Wall Street Blues.
I've tried to make sure this game is as easy to install as possible. All
you have to do is uncrunch the file Pug.Lzx in your pfiles directory. It
sets up the rest when the game is run the first time.

	Next you will have to adopt the file 'intro' into your pfiles as an 
Arexx program. It's better to label this game as "Pugilistic Glory 2.0 (Boxing). 
Some people may not know what a pugilist is.

Next go into Cnet's config screen for EVENTS and list the program Box.maint 
to run as an Arexx program at midnight. This program will ensure that the 
players age, so one fighter doesn't stay the champion too long. The
fighters are automatically retired at the age of 45.

	Next go into the NEWS portion of your BBS and adopt the file
Pugglo.advertisement as a text file. This will tell your users that the
game has been added to your system. The news portion is great because the
users are only told once.

	Next take a text editor and edit the file total.data. This file
contains the number of users you would expect to have on your BBS. It must
be set above the highest account number owned on your BBS. You may want to
set it up about 50 higher to allow for new users. The use of this number is
to search the different accounts in the game. If you have 900 users on your
BBS then some functions may take a long time to complete. The lower this
number is the faster it will run, however it must be above the highest account
number on your BBS.

	There are two log files that need to be kept trimmed. I've included
the file trimlog I picked up somewhere that does this quite well. It leaves
the last so many lines of the logs and cuts the rest off the top. I did not
program this. It is included for your convienence. An example pic of it's
use is included. The files needed to be trimmed are;

Pfiles:Box/hist
Pfiles:Box/Retired
Pfiles:Box/fightlist

	Trimlog does have some limitations on the file size. I forget what
it is but your safe setting it to 50 lines.

	Now what could be more simpler than that?

SYSOP MENU:

	The SysOp menu can only be accessed by user number 1. Can't have
everyone changing the game setup. At the main menu you will see the listing
goes up to 10, 10 being to quit the game. At this menu the SysOp may enter
the number 21 and access the SysOp menu. At this menu you can do the
following;

	Retire A Fighter: I've tested this and as of the release date it
was working good. As well as a player using retirement. It may be needed
for some SysOps, so I put it in there.

	Reset The Pros: This resets the Pros Win Loss records to 0. To
reset their fight record, you must go in and delete them manually. New
records will be made for them when they fight again. This option does take
time to complete as it resets records for 32 Pros.

	Reset The Entire Game: This deletes all players and their records.
It also deletes the Pros records and resets their Win Loss records.

	Create a Cyber Boxer: Assigns a fighter (you pick the account
number, an unused one) and makes it so players will have more fighters to 
fight.  These boxers do not challenge anyone, however if they are good 
they could become champions when the current champions retire. These 
fighters will get old. Caution. do not add too many computer players. 
I recommend three, one of each style.


                              HAVE FUN!



